<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_kinematic_configurations_differential_drive" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Kinematic Configurations and Differential Drive</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Kinematics studies motion without modeling the forces that produce it. In robotics, kinematic models let us predict translation, turning, and final position from geometry and wheel motion. These models are used from VEX platforms to industrial and service robots.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_diff_drive_basics">
    <title>Differential Drive Basics</title>
    <p>A differential drive robot has two independently driven wheels plus passive support wheels. It turns by changing relative left and right wheel speeds rather than steering front wheels.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Straight motion:</em> left and right wheel speeds are equal.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Right turn:</em> left wheel faster than right wheel.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Left turn:</em> right wheel faster than left wheel.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">In-place rotation:</em> wheels spin in opposite directions.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_track_width">
    <title>Track Width and Turning Behavior</title>
    <p>Track width $L$ is the distance between wheel centers. For the same wheel speeds, a smaller $L$ yields tighter turns, while a larger $L$ produces wider turning radius.</p>
    <p>This explains why two robots with different drivetrains can follow visibly different curves even under similar motor commands.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_diff_drive_examples">
    <title>Examples</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Warehouse robots:</em> Differential drive supports reliable path following and obstacle avoidance through independent wheel control.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Robotic vacuums:</em> The same wheel-speed logic enables turning around furniture and rotating out of corners.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>VEX autonomous navigation uses the same kinematic principles at smaller scale.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_diff_drive_checks">
    <title>Concept Checks</title>
    <ol>
      <li><p>A differential drive robot moves straight when left and right wheel speeds are equal.</p></li>
      <li><p>To turn right, command the left side faster than the right side.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>
</section>
